WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:05.81
In part one, an Owner submitted a direction and Hewn queued it.

00:00:05.81 --> 00:00:10.52
In part two, the direction actually runs: a worker leases each stage,

00:00:10.52 --> 00:00:15.72
from zero to ten, in order, and Mission Control follows along live until the

00:00:15.72 --> 00:00:18.87
direction is Complete and the project is Idle.

00:00:20.02 --> 00:00:22.25
Three pieces meet in this slice.

00:00:22.25 --> 00:00:26.44
The API gains the pipeline orchestrator and a lease service.

00:00:26.44 --> 00:00:31.89
A new TypeScript worker leases runs, sends heartbeats and reports checkpoints.

00:00:31.89 --> 00:00:36.42
And the project page in Mission Control listens to a SignalR hub,

00:00:36.42 --> 00:00:39.71
so every stage change appears without a reload.

00:00:40.06 --> 00:00:42.59
The worker doesn't call a model yet.

00:00:42.59 --> 00:00:46.19
It runs a scripted executor: three steps per stage,

00:00:46.19 --> 00:00:48.51
with no agent and no GitHub call.

00:00:48.51 --> 00:00:54.22
That keeps tests and demos deterministic, and later parts swap in the real stages

00:00:54.22 --> 00:00:55.20
one at a time.

00:00:56.35 --> 00:00:59.27
Here is the advance sequence from the design.

00:00:59.27 --> 00:01:02.06
When a worker reports that a run succeeded,

00:01:02.06 --> 00:01:07.18
the orchestrator marks the stage Succeeded and queues a run for the next stage,

00:01:07.18 --> 00:01:09.97
all in one commit with the matching events.

00:01:09.97 --> 00:01:14.51
After stage ten, it marks the direction Complete and the project Idle.

00:01:14.86 --> 00:01:17.16
A lease is one conditional update.

00:01:17.16 --> 00:01:22.50
The run moves from Queued to Leased only if no other run of the same project is

00:01:22.50 --> 00:01:25.54
leased, so one project never has two workers.

00:01:25.54 --> 00:01:27.91
Every grant raises the run's epoch.

00:01:27.91 --> 00:01:32.17
A worker that missed sixty seconds of heartbeats loses the run,

00:01:32.17 --> 00:01:36.77
and any call it makes with the old epoch is refused with lease lost.

00:01:37.92 --> 00:01:39.58
The worker loop is small.

00:01:39.58 --> 00:01:42.71
It leases a run, starts a ten-second heartbeat,

00:01:42.71 --> 00:01:47.11
and walks the steps after the last checkpoint, reporting each one.

00:01:47.11 --> 00:01:50.71
If the lease is lost, or two heartbeats in a row fail,

00:01:50.71 --> 00:01:52.98
it stops and reports nothing more.

00:01:53.33 --> 00:01:57.64
In Mission Control, the API library owns the hub connection.

00:01:57.64 --> 00:02:03.39
It replays any events the page missed by sequence number, then streams the rest.

00:02:03.39 --> 00:02:06.55
The page folds each event into its snapshot.

00:02:07.70 --> 00:02:13.36
The backend gained fifteen acceptance tests: stages in order, one lease per project,

00:02:13.36 --> 00:02:18.34
expiry after sixty seconds with the checkpoint kept, stale epochs refused,

00:02:18.34 --> 00:02:20.23
and live events in sequence.

00:02:20.23 --> 00:02:23.46
The worker has sixteen tests against a fake API,

00:02:23.46 --> 00:02:26.42
with fake timers for heartbeats and polling.

00:02:26.42 --> 00:02:31.47
Playwright now starts real workers and watches a direction run to Complete.

00:02:32.62 --> 00:02:37.60
Here is the real app, with one scripted worker running in the background.

00:02:37.60 --> 00:02:41.69
The Owner names the project, describes the idea and submits.

00:02:41.69 --> 00:02:46.66
The project page opens, and within a moment the worker leases stage zero.

00:02:46.66 --> 00:02:50.82
From there, stages tick from Pending to Running to Succeeded,

00:02:50.82 --> 00:02:54.02
each one starting only after the one before it,

00:02:54.02 --> 00:02:57.50
and the running stage's timer counts up as it goes.

00:02:57.50 --> 00:03:01.11
Nobody reloads the page and nobody approves anything.

00:03:01.11 --> 00:03:06.84
When stage ten finishes, the header reads Complete and the callout confirms that all

00:03:06.84 --> 00:03:08.48
eleven stages succeeded.

00:03:08.83 --> 00:03:14.20
The running stage is marked as the current step, with a spinner and a text label,

00:03:14.20 --> 00:03:16.46
so status never depends on colour.

00:03:16.46 --> 00:03:20.51
Screen readers hear each change through a polite live region.

00:03:20.51 --> 00:03:24.23
On a phone and in dark mode the pipeline reads the same.

00:03:25.38 --> 00:03:31.06
Directions now run end to end on a worker, fenced by leases and shown live.

00:03:31.06 --> 00:03:37.41
Next, stage zero does real work: it creates the repository and installs the Greenlit

00:03:37.41 --> 00:03:37.94
skills.
